 "verdict": "GRID: 33 ablation arms, s in [0.3998, 0.8782] against the mean-field critical 1/r = 0.3333; measured lambda in [+0.0115, +0.4183]; predicted lambda_MF in [+0.1819, +0.9687]. PRIMARY: mean field's predicted lambda spans 0.7868 across these 33 interventions while the measured exponent spans 0.4068 -- a range ratio of 1.934 against the 0.5 a predictor needs to be informative in its target's units. Spearman(s, lambda) = +0.2958 (p = 0.0947). NULL, WITH POWER: the predictor has room to be wrong (ratio 1.934, against a 0.5 gate) and it is not detectably wrong -- rho = +0.2958 at p = 0.0947 is a weak positive consistent with zero, in the direction mean field predicts. This is neither the falsification the three-arm pilot suggested nor a confirmation: the pilot's apparent anti-correlation was an artifact of measuring three arms whose s happened to span 0.06 of the 0.48 the full grid covers. Gates: dynamic_range: measured lambda_ca across arms spans 0.4068 against its own noise floor 0.06565 (6.20x, gate 2.0x); distinct_units: 33 sampled ablation arms collapse to 33 distinct (gate 12) BOUNDARY: one model, one checkpoint, one radius, one fixed ensemble. lambda_ca is read from F79/F80 rather than re-measured, so this inherits their geometry exactly and nothing here re-opens their numbers. A failure of ANNEALED mean field is not a failure of single-token sensitivity as such -- F94's rung 2 got 17 of 19 ECA rules right, missing rule 232, MAJORITY, the canonical canalizing function.",
